Merge/split energy instrumentation for GIZMO (diagnostic only).

Re-applies the MERGE_SPLIT_ENERGY_DIAGNOSTIC tallies that were hoisted out of merge_particles_ij.
Three cumulative, MPI-reduced reports, printed once per merge_and_split_particles call:

  Merge energy budget  dE      change in the TRUE state (InternalEnergy + KE from P[].Vel)
                       dE_pred same for the PREDICTED state (InternalEnergyPred + VelPred), which is
                               what the hydro integrates -- these differ, and that mattered
                       KE_com  (1/2)*mu*|dv|^2, what a momentum-conserving merge must dissipate
                       resid   the correction merge_particles_ij computes
                       dE+KE_com vanishes iff the discarded COM-frame KE is the only error
  Merge Pred floor     how much the InternalEnergyPred >= 0.5*InternalEnergy clamp injects, and how
                       often. That clamp is one-directional (can only add energy), so it looks like a
                       suspect; the tally is how you rule it out.
  Merge pair timing    TimeBin distribution of merged pairs and whether Ti_current differs, i.e.
                       whether the survivor inherits mass integrated on another clock.

    git apply scripts/merge_energy_diagnostic.patch      # or: git apply -3

WHAT IT ESTABLISHED, in the adiabatic wind_singlestar test where E_kin+E_th = L*t exactly:
  - The merge discard costs ~23% of the injected energy, and MERGE_SPLIT_CONSERVE_ENERGY removes it
    exactly: dE falls to roundoff with resid == KE_com identically.
  - dE_pred was large while dE was ~0, because the residual was computed from the true state and then
    added to BOTH books -- which cannot balance the predicted one, since VelPred != Vel mid-step. Fixed
    by giving the predicted state its own residual.
  - The Pred floor never fires at all here. Do not go hunting there.
  - Merged pairs always share Ti_current, so no merge grafts on mass integrated against another clock.
    That is structural: merging runs inside the drift, after every participant has been drifted to the
    current time. Most pairs DO differ in TimeBin and the survivor keeps its own bin, but restricting
    merges to same-bin pairs was tried and made conservation worse -- the veto redirects merges to
    worse-matched partners rather than suppressing them, and partner quality matters more than the bin.

A residual few percent remains. It is merge-associated but is NOT a bookkeeping error: at the merge
instant the true energy, the predicted energy and the mass all balance to roundoff, and the deficit is
insensitive to CourantFac, so it is not temporal truncation error either. It scales with how badly
matched the merged partners are. Not chased further.

